Aälsa	A woman associated with House Gaunum, presumed to have served as a concubine in its household during the same period as Serwë.
Abbarsallas	Mimara's owner during her first five years in Carythusal.
Aelswë	The mortal daughter of Gilgaöl in ancient Sakarpi legend, remembered in a tale involving the Sakarpi king Suberd.
Aëngelas|Aënga	A Werigdan warrior, husband of Valrissa and father of Bengulla.
Aethelarius VI|Athullara	The Norsirai king of Atrithau and last ruler in the line of Morghund. His name is the Sheyic form of Athullara.
Aethiola	The High-Priestess of the Cult of Yatwer.
Agnaras	A captain in the Imperial Army and follower of Ikurei Conphas.
Aisarinqu	A Siölan Cûnuroi, wife of Cinial'jin and presumed mother of Immiriccas, Aisralu, and Enpiralas.
Aisralu	A Siölan Cûnuroi, daughter of Cinial'jin and presumed daughter of Aisarinqu; Immiriccas and Enpiralas are her brothers.
Ajencis|The Great Kyranean	A Ketyai philosopher born in Mehtsonc, celebrated as the father of syllogistic logic and algebra and regarded by many as the greatest philosopher. His critical philosophy has a consistently skeptical core, questioning beliefs founded on human weakness rather than reason or the world. Recognized as a prodigy, he received the Kyranean institution of Protection from the High King at eight years old, granting him freedom to speak without fear of reprisal. His writings include The Syllogistics, Meta-Analytics, Theophysics, and several Analytics of Men.
Ajokli|The Four-Horned Brother	The god of thievery and deception, represented in scripture as either a mischievous companion or a cruel rival of other gods. Although named among the primary Gods in The Chronicle of the Tusk, he has no formal cult; his devotees instead form an informal network across the great cities of the Three Seas. The Mar’eddat names him as Gierra's faithless husband.
Akkeägni|God of a Thousand Hands	The god of disease and a Bellicose God who favors those who struggle against his influence rather than flatterers and worshippers. His priesthood is consequently the Three Seas' principal repository of physicians. Its priests employ pharmaka and reliquaries against illness, sound small prayer chimes in thanks, and may bear devotional tattoos.
Akori Kumrezzer|Akori Kumrezzar	The Ketyai Palatine of Kutapileth in High Ainon and one of the leaders of the Vulgar Holy War.
Algari	An elderly Cironji Ketyai who serves as body-slave to Prince Nersei Proyas.
Amakûnir	A Kûniüric lord who took part in the Second Ordeal at the Battle of Eleneöt Fields.
Amarsla	One of Esmenet's body-slaves.
Amrezzer the Black|Amrezzer	A legendary Surartu Grandmaster of the Scarlet Spires, responsible for securing the river fortress of Kiz in Far Antique Carythusal.
Amurrei Ganyatti	A Ketyai nobleman who holds the Conriyan title of Palatine of Ankirioth.
Anagkë|The Whore of Fate|The Whore	The goddess of fortune and one of the principal Compensatory Gods, rewarding mortal devotion with paradise in the afterlife. Her cult is especially popular among the higher political castes of the Three Seas. Old Kiünnat tradition calls her the younger sister of Bukris, god of famine, and she is also associated with the stars and favorable conjunctions.
Anarlû	A presumably mythical figure mentioned in one of the Boatman's songs.
Anasûrimbor Celmomas II|White Lord of Trysë	The last and greatest High King of Kûniüri and leader of the coalition against Golgotterath during the early Apocalypse. A close friend of the sorcerer Seswatha, who often advised him, he was married to Suriala and counted Ganrelka II and the renowned warrior Nau-Cayûti among his sons. He wielded Glimir, a large ensorcelled sword said to pass through foes with ease.
Anasûrimbor Ganrelka II	The heir and successor of Anasûrimbor Celmomas II, a participant in the Second Ordeal, and the last reigning High King of Kûniüri.
Anasûrimbor Huörmomas	A Norsirai of Kûniüri and the twin brother of Anasûrimbor Celmomas II, remembered in The Sagas.
Anasûrimbor Inrilatas|Inri	A Prince-Imperial of the New Empire, the second son and fifth child of Kellhus and Esmenet. Half Dûnyain and half Ketyai, he inherited an exceptional measure of his father's traits but was kept secluded within the Andiamine Heights because of his profound instability. He has a long face, blue eyes, golden hair, and a muscular build.
Anasûrimbor Kayûtas|Kayû	The first son and eldest child of Kellhus and Esmenet, a Prince-Imperial and General of the Kidruhil. Born in Shimeh and named for Anasûrimbor Nau-Cayûti, he was raised alongside Moënghus II as a fraternal twin. Tall and slender, with blond hair, blue eyes, a long face, and a plaited beard, he closely resembles his father.
Anasûrimbor Kellhus|Kellhus|Prince of Nothing	A Norsirai Dûnyain monk of the Anasûrimbor line, known as the Prince of Nothing and identified as the son of Anasûrimbor Moënghus.
Anasûrimbor Kelmomas|Kel	A Prince-Imperial of the New Empire, the third son and sixth child of Kellhus and Esmenet, and the twin brother of Samarmas. Half Dûnyain and half Ketyai, he was named after Anasûrimbor Celmomas II.
Anasûrimbor Koringhus	A Norsirai Dûnyain monk, son of Anasûrimbor Kellhus, and father of the child known as the Boy.
Anasûrimbor Moënghus	A Norsirai former Dûnyain monk and the father of Anasûrimbor Kellhus.
Anasûrimbor Moënghus II	The eldest of the Prince-Imperials and the son of Serwë.
Anasûrimbor Mygella|Hero-King	A famed Norsirai High King of Aörsi whose deeds are recounted in The Sagas.
Anasûrimbor Nanor-Mikhus	A High King of Aörsi who commissioned the fortress of Dagliash.
Anasûrimbor Nanor-Ukkerja I|Nanor-Ukkerja I|Hammer of Heaven	The first Anasûrimbor High King and founder of Kûniüri, established after his defeat of the Scintya. His Kûniüric epithet derives from the Ûmeritic nanar hukisha. He expanded Kûniüri from the Yimaleti Mountains to the Cerish Sea and from Sakarpus to the Demua Mountains, beginning what scholars regard as the longest-reigning dynasty in recorded history.
Anasûrimbor Nanor-Ukkerja VI	A High King of Aörsi who ordered the abandonment of all ninety-nine Hooded Redoubts.
Anasûrimbor Nau-Cayûti|Nau-Cayûti|Cayû|Blessed Son|Murswagga|Dragonslayer	A prince of Kûniüri, officially the youngest son of Celmomas II and Suriala, and a hero renowned for martial brilliance during Kûniüri's struggle against the Consult. At the Battle of Ossirish he routed the Consult forces and slew Tanhafut the Red, earning the name Murswagga, the Dragonslayer. His exploits are celebrated in The Sagas.
Anasûrimbor Nimeric	A Norsirai High King of ancient Aörsi during the era of the Apocalypse.
Anasûrimbor Omindalea	A Norsirai woman of the Ûmeri Empire, the first daughter of Sanna-Neorjë of House Anasûrimbor and mother of Sanna-Jephera.
Anasûrimbor Samarmas|Sammi	A Prince-Imperial of the New Empire, the fourth son and seventh and youngest child of Kellhus and Esmenet, and the twin brother of Kelmomas. He is half Dûnyain and half Ketyai.
Anasûrimbor Sanna-Jephera|Twoheart	An Ûmeri Man–Nonman hybrid, son of Anasûrimbor Omindalea and the Nonman Siqû Jiricet. He was formally adopted as the heir of his grandfather, Anasûrimbor Sanna-Neorjë.
Anasûrimbor Sanna-Neorjë	A Norsirai nobleman who headed House Anasûrimbor in the Ûmeri Empire. He was the father of Omindalea and formally adopted his grandson Sanna-Jephera as his heir.
Anasûrimbor Serwa|Ladywitch|Serri|Serwa Memirrû	A Princess-Imperial of the New Empire, the second daughter and third child of Kellhus and Esmenet. Named after Serwë, she is Grandmistress of the Swayal Sisterhood, the only school for sorceresses in Eärwa.
Anasûrimbor Theliopa|Thelli	A half-Dûnyain, half-Ketyai Princess-Imperial of the New Empire, the eldest daughter and second child of Kellhus and Esmenet. She serves as an Imperial Adviser in Momemn.
Anaxophus V|Anakka	A Ketyai High King of Kyraneas during the Apocalypse and a close friend of the sorcerer Seswatha, who called him Anakka. He was entrusted with the Heron Spear, a weapon central to the struggle against the No-God.
Anchilas	The artist who created the celebrated fresco known as the Blue Hubris.
Angeshraël|Burnt Prophet	The most celebrated Old Prophet of the Tusk, credited with leading the Five Tribes of Men into Eärwa. He is called the Burnt Prophet because, when confronted by Husyelt at Mount Eshki, he obeyed the god by bowing his face into his fire. His wife was Esmenet, with whom he had a son named Oresh.
Anissi	A half-Scylvendi, half-Norsirai woman of the Utemot and the favorite wife of Cnaiür urs Skiötha. Their children include a daughter named Sanathi. Cnaiür prizes Anissi above his other wives for enduring his harshness while continuing to love him.
Ankaryotis	A Ciphrang—a demonic agency of the Outside—and one of the more manageable Potents controlled by the Scarlet Spires. In material form it is a writhing abomination with knife-like scales, limbs compared to iron pillars, an elephantine head, and a vast mouth encircled by eyes.
Ankharlus	A famed Norsirai commentator from Kûniüri and high priest of Gilgaöl.
Ansacer ab Salajka	The Sapatishah-Governor of Gedea, whose totem is the Black Gazelle.
Anzumarapata II	The Nilnameshi king of Invishi during the fourteenth century of the Year-of-the-Tusk.
Apperens Saccarees	Grandmaster of the Mandate during the Great Ordeal and an Exalt-Magus. Apart from Kellhus and Serwa, he is the only sorcerer rumored to have successfully recited a metagnostic cant.
Aratrula the Mad|Aratrula	A Meöri High King who commissioned the Nausk Mausoleum to protect his soul from the Outside.
Areamanteras	A Nansur general who commands the Nasueret Column of the Imperial Army and follows Ikurei Conphas.
Arishal	One of Anasûrimbor Kellhus's bodyguards, mentioned by Nersei Proyas in a letter to his father, Nersei Eukernas II.
Arithmeas	The portly Nansur Prime Augur to Ikurei Xerius III and the emperor's favored augur and astrologer.
Arnyalsa	A famed missionary priest of the Three Seas.
Ars Sibbul	The author of Six Ontonomies.
Arweal	A Tydonni Norsirai, client thane of Earl Rilding Werijen Greatheart, and one of the Nascenti.
Aspa Memkumri	An Ainoni Ketyai, client of Sirpal Uranyanka, and member of the Holy War.
Asward	A member of the Skin Eaters.
Aulisi	A concubine of Anasûrimbor Nau-Cayûti, remembered in accounts surrounding the ancient struggle against Golgotterath.
Aulyanau	One of the leaders of the Cond tribesmen during the Second Age.
Aulyanau Cawa-Imvullar	An emperor of the Pseudo-Ûmeri Empire, known primarily for his defeat by the Scintya.
Aurang|Iskiak|Sarpanur|Sin-Pharion|Angel of Deceit|Warlord	An Inchoroi prince, twin brother of Aurax, and one of the original founders of the Consult. He served as Spear-Bearer to King Sil and as Horde-General to the No-God during the Apocalypse. Different peoples remember him as King-Healer, the Angel of Deceit, and the Warlord.
Aurax	An Inchoroi prince, ranking Consult member, and twin brother of Aurang. Mandate scholars speculate that he first taught the science of the Tekne to the Mangaecca. Lacking the vocal apparatus for speech, he communicates through pheromones and telepathy.
Aurili	One of the Cûnuroi remaining in Ishterebinth by 4132.
Avalunsil|The Twice-Flowered	A legendary Norsirai empress of the Ûmeri Empire in Far Antiquity and daughter of Wulta-Ongorean.
Avarartu	A Nilnameshi lord of the Great Ordeal.
Balait urs Kututha|Bala	A Scylvendi warrior of the Utemot tribe, son of Kututha and brother-in-law of Cnaiür urs Skiötha.
Bannut urs Hannut	A Scylvendi warrior of the Utemot tribes, son of Hannut, brother of Skiötha urs Hannut, and uncle of Cnaiür urs Skiötha.
Bansipitas	An Ordealman from Sepa-Gielgath.
Baribul	A member of the Company of Scions in the Great Ordeal.
Bastion	One of the Skin Eaters' mules, named by Mimara.
Baxatas	A Nansur general who commands the Selial Column during the Holy War.
Belmorn	A member of the Stone Hags.
Bengulla	The Werigdan Norsirai son of Aëngelas and Valrissa.
Beotha Tuthorsa	A Tydonni Norsirai soldier, member of the Holy War, and devout believer in the divinity of Anasûrimbor Kellhus.
Beotha Tûtmor	An Ordealman and New Imperial Consul of Ce Tydonn.
Biaxi Coronsas	Patridomos of House Biaxi during the events of The Prince of Nothing.
Biaxi Sankas	Patridomos of House Biaxi during the Aspect-Emperor era and an important member of the New Congregate.
Biaxi Scoulas	A Nansur nobleman and the second Knight-Commander of the Shrial Knights during the Holy War.
Biaxi Sompas	A Nansur general of the Kidruhil and eldest son of Biaxi Coronsas, Patridomos of House Biaxi.
Biaxi Tarpellas	General of the Nansur contingent of the Great Ordeal.
Bompothur	A Nilnameshi cook employed in the Andiamine Heights.
Borswelka I	A king of the Meöri Empire whose reign began in 1021.
Borswelka II	A king of the Meöri Empire and grandson of Borswelka I.
Borswelka IV	A king of the Meöri Empire and father of Engûs.
The Boy	An unnamed Norsirai initiate of the Dûnyain, son of Anasûrimbor Koringhus and grandson of Anasûrimbor Kellhus.
Braelwan Cynnea	A Galeoth Norsirai nobleman and Earl of Agmundr during the Holy War.
Bukris	The god of famine and one of the Punitive Gods, who command sacrifice through threats and suffering. He has no formal cult or priesthood. Kiünnat tradition names him as Anagkë's elder brother, so Anagkean priests administer his rites of propitiation during times of hunger.
Burulan	A Kianene woman who serves as one of Esmenet's body-slaves.
Byantas	A Norsirai writer of the Ceneian Empire in Near Antiquity.
Byrikki|The Candlemaker	A fanatical Ordealman known as the Candlemaker.
Calasthenes	An Ainoni sorcerer of rank in the Scarlet Spires.
Callustras	An elderly soldier and customer of Esmenet.
Carindûsû	The Nilnameshi Ketyai Grandmaster of the Vokalati, the feared sorcerous School known as the Sun-wailers of Nilnamesh.
Carû-Ignaini	A Trysean God-King remembered for his jealousy toward Tynwur.
Carû-Ongonean	The third Norsirai God-King of the Ûmeri Empire during the Nonman Tutelage and founder of the original Library of Sauglish.
Casidas	A famed philosopher and historian of Near Antiquity, best known for his authoritative work The Annals of Cenei.
Caxes Anthirul	An Exalt-General stationed in Momemn during the Great Ordeal.
Cel-Ongonean	A ruler of the Ûmeri Empire who faced Aulyanau the Conqueror at the Battle of River Axau.
Cememketri	The Shiropti Grandmaster of the Imperial Saik during the Holy War.
Cet’ingira|Mekeritrig|Mantraitor|The Evil Siqû	An Erratic Nonman Siqû and powerful Qûya, known in Kûniüric as Mekeritrig, the Traitor of Men. He revealed the location of Min-Uroikas to the Mangaecca and became a ranking member of the Consult during the Apocalypse.
Cet’moyol	A Cûnuroi prince of Siöl, son of King Cu'huriol and father of Cû'jara-Cinmoi.
Charamemas	A famed Shrial commentator, author of The Ten Holies, and tutor in exoterics to Nersei Proyas.
Charampa	A prince of Cingulat and member of the Company of Scions in the Great Ordeal.
Chassain	A Galeoth caste-noble who serves as a Judge in the Great Ordeal.
Chepheramunni	The Ainoni King-Regent of High Ainon and nominal leader of the Ainoni during much of the First Holy War.
Chiki|Pomegranate	An orphaned Ainoni Ketyai boy employed as a runner by Drusas Achamian in High Ainon. Outside that service, he survives through prostitution.
Cilcûliccas|Lord of Swans	An Injori Qûya called the Lord of Swans, renowned for confronting Murathaur the Silver.
Cimoira	The first recorded child of mixed Man and Cûnuroi parentage, as preserved in the Isûphiryas. A daughter of the Emwama man Sirwitta, she was accepted and raised among the Cûnuroi.
Cinganjehoi ab Sakjal|Tiger of Eumarna	A famed Kianene Ketyai Sapatishah-Governor of Eumarna, known among his people as the Tiger of Eumarna, and father of Inrilil ab Cinganjehoi.
Cinial'jin	A well-known Erratic Nonman and father of Immiriccas. The Four Revelations of Cinial’jin is told from his point of view.
Ciögli|Ciögli the Mountain|Mountain	A legendary Siölan Ishroi and the strongest of the Nonmen, renowned for his struggles against great foes during the Cûno-Inchoroi Wars.
Clia Saxillas	Captain of the Inchausti during the Great Ordeal.
Cnaiür urs Skiötha	A Scylvendi warrior and chieftain of the Utemot tribe.
Cnaiür urs Skiötha's mother	The unnamed first wife of Skiötha urs Hannut and mother of Cnaiür urs Skiötha.
Coithus Agabon	An Ordealman and the youngest son of Coithus Narnol.
Coithus Asilda	Daughter of Coithus Eryeat, sister of Coithus Narnol and Coithus Saubon, and mother of Coithus Athjeäri.
Coithus Athjeäri|Hurall’arkeet|Wind Has Teeth	The Galeoth Earl of Gaenri and nephew of Coithus Saubon. Regarded as the eyes of the Holy War, he scouts ahead with his men and reports on the Fanim.
Coithus Eryeat	The Norsirai king of Galeoth during the Holy War and father of Coithus Narnol, Coithus Saubon, and Coithus Asilda.
Coithus Narnol	An Ordealman and Believer-King of Galeoth, eldest surviving son of Coithus Eryeat and brother of Coithus Saubon and Coithus Asilda.
Coithus Saubon|The Blond Beast	The seventh son of King Coithus Eryeat of Galeoth and titular leader of the Galeoth contingent in the First Holy War.
Cojirani ab Houk	The Kianene Grandee of Mizrai, famed for his enormous strength and size.
Conger	A member of the Skin Eaters.
Couras Nantilla	The Earl of Pikka and general of the Cengemi in the Great Ordeal.
Cratianas	The author of Nilnameshi Lore.
Cuäxaji	The Kianene Ketyai Sapatishah-Governor of Khemema.
Cu’cirrurn	One of the Cûnuroi remaining in Ishterebinth by 4132.
Cu’cûlol	A Siölan Nonman Qûya, member of the Consult, and kinsman of Cû'jara-Cinmoi.
Cu’huriol	The Cûnuroi king of Siöl before Arkfall, father of Cet'moyol and Linqirû, and grandfather of Cû'jara-Cinmoi.
Cû’jara-Cinmoi|The Copper Tree of Siöl	The greatest of the Nonman kings, High King of Siöl, and first great foe of the Inchoroi. He is the son of Cet'moyol and Linqirû and husband of Hanalinqû.
Cu’mimiral|Dragon-gored|Lord Limper	An Injori Ishroi known as Dragon-gored and the Lord Limper.
Cûncari	A Nonman remembered and called out for by Cleric in Cil-Aujas.
Cundraul III	A High King of Akksersia mentioned in The Sagas.
Cûnwerishau	The first God-King of Trysë, famed for conquering the cities of the River Aumris and forging the first treaty between Men and Nonmen.
Cutias Pansulla	The New Imperial Consul of Nansur and a political rival of Esmenet.
Cutias Sarcellus	A Nansur Ketyai Knight-Commander of the Shrial Knights during the Holy War.
Cwithar Ottma	A Tydonni Norsirai thane and one of the Nascenti.
Damergal	The Tydonni Norsirai Earl of Cuärweth and a member of the Holy War.
Damial’isharin	A Siölan Ishroi who, after observing an itinerant Sranc camp from concealment, famously argued that the Sranc possessed social customs and regimes nearly as complex as those of the Cûnuroi.
Darastius	A sorcerer of the Imperial Saik.
Davdûl	A Mihtrûlic Grandmaster regarded as the most gifted sorcerous artificer among Men.
Daybreak	Drusas Achamian's mule.
Denthuel	A Sakarpi baron.
Diagol	Shriah of the Thousand Temples from 3371 to 3383, notorious for cruel excesses. His reign prompted the high clergy to issue the Writ of Psata-Antyu, limiting the authority of future Shriahs.
Dinchases|Bloody Dinch	A captain of Attrempus and lifelong comrade-in-arms to Krijates Xinemus.
Diremti brothers	Two of Sanumnis's Chorae bowmen.
Dogora Teör	The Tydonni Norsirai Earl of Sumagalt and a member of the Holy War.
Domilli	The author of Rudiments.
Droettal	A Sakarpi priest of Gilgaöl.
Drusas Achamian|Akka	A Nroni Ketyai former Mandate Schoolman and spy. During the Holy War he investigates the Scarlet Spires and the Thousand Temples. He bears the titles Holy Tutor, Vizier, and Wizard.
Dun Heörsa	A Galeoth Norsirai former thane and Shield-Captain of the Hundred Pillars.
Dunjoksha	The Sapatishah-Governor of Holy Amoteu, regarded by the Inrithi as a tyrant.
